<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="sec-equations-of-lines-and-planes">
  <title>Definitions and examples</title>

  <introduction>

  <definition>
    <title>Linear equations</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        An equation of the form
        <me>a_1x_1 + a_2x_2 + \cdots + a_nx_n = b</me>
        where <m>a_1,\dots,a_n,b</m> are scalars, and <m>x_1,\dots,x_n</m> are variables is called a <term>linear equation</term>. The real quantities <m>a_1,\ldots,a_n</m> are called <term>coefficients</term>, <m>b</m> is called a <term>constant</term>, while the variables <m>x_1,\ldots,x_n</m> are called <em>unknowns</em>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="def-properties-of-linear-systems">
    <title>Systems of linear equations</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <term>system of linear equations</term> or <term>linear system</term> is a set of <m>m</m> linear equations each in <m>n</m> unknowns. A linear system has the form

        <men xml:id="me-1-1-linear-equation-archetype">
          \begin{array}{ccccccccc}
          a_{11}x_1 \amp + \amp a_{12}x_2 \amp + \amp \cdots \amp + \amp a_{1n}x_n \amp = \amp b_1    \\
          a_{21}x_1 \amp + \amp a_{22}x_2 \amp + \amp \cdots \amp + \amp a_{2n}x_n \amp = \amp b_2    \\
          \vdots    \amp   \amp \vdots    \amp   \amp        \amp   \amp \vdots    \amp   \amp \vdots \\
          a_{m1}x_1 \amp + \amp a_{m2}x_2 \amp + \amp \cdots \amp + \amp a_{mn}x_n \amp = \amp b_m
          \end{array}
        </men>

        where <m>a_{11},\dots,a_{mn}</m> and <m>b_1,\dots,b_m</m> are constants.
      </p>

      <p>
        A <term>solution</term> to the linear system <xref ref="me-1-1-linear-equation-archetype"/> is a sequence of <m>n</m> numbers <m>s_1,\ldots,s_n</m> so that <em>each</em> equation in <xref ref="me-1-1-linear-equation-archetype"/> is satisfied when the substitutions <m>x_1 = s_1</m>, <m>x_2=s_2,\ldots,x_n=s_n</m> are made.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <warning xml:id="warn-u2-linear-system-variables">
    <title>Variable names</title>
    <p>
      We use <m>x,y,z</m> in geometric examples and <m>x_1,\dots,x_n</m> for general systems.
    </p>
  </warning>

  <activity>
    <title>Nonlinear equations (U2-LO1)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What are some equations that are not linear equations?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Here are four examples:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><m>x^2 + y = 1</m> is nonlinear because of the squared term <m>x^2</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><m>xy = 2</m> is nonlinear because of the product of variables <m>xy</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><m>\sin(x) + y = 0</m> is nonlinear because of the sine function <m>\sin</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><m>\sqrt{x} + y = 1</m> is nonlinear because of the square root <m>\sqrt{x}</m>.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <p>
    Before introducing a general algorithm, we look at a few small systems where the equations can be analyzed directly.
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="ex-1-1-forest-animals-1">
    <title>Forest animals (U2-LO1, U2-LO2)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In a Wisconsin forest, there are robins and badgers. Together they have <m>18</m> heads and <m>56</m> legs. How many robins and badgers are in the forest?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m>x</m> be the number of robins, and <m>y</m> the number of badgers.
      </p>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Heads</title>
          <m>x + y = 18</m>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Legs</title>
          <m>2x + 4y = 56</m>
        </li>
      </dl>
      <p>
        Rearranging the first equation, we find <m>y = 18-x</m>. Substituting this into the second equation:
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>2x + 4(18-x) \amp = 56</mrow>
          <mrow>2x + 72 - 4x \amp = 56</mrow>
          <mrow>-2x \amp = -16</mrow>
          <mrow>x \amp = 8</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Since <m>y = 18 - x</m> and <m>x = 8</m>, <m>y = 10</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        So there are <m>8</m> robins in the forest, and <m>10</m> badgers in the forest.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <title>Identifying coefficients, constants, and unknowns (U2-LO1)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Identify the coefficients, the constants and the unknowns in each linear equation in <xref ref="ex-1-1-forest-animals-1"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <title>
          Table of coefficients, constants, and unknowns.
        </title>

        <tabular>

          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Equation</cell>
            <cell>Coefficients</cell>
            <cell>Constants</cell>
            <cell>Unknowns</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><m>x + y = 18</m></cell>
            <cell><m>1,1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>18</m></cell>
            <cell><m>x,y</m></cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><m>2x + 4y = 56</m></cell>
            <cell><m>2,4</m></cell>
            <cell><m>56</m></cell>
            <cell><m>x,y</m></cell>
          </row>

        </tabular>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <definition xml:id="def-u2-consistency-equivalence">
    <title>Consistency and equivalence</title>
    <statement>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>If a linear system has <em>no</em> solution, it is called <term>inconsistent</term>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>If a linear system has at least one solution, it is called <term>consistent</term>. Note that an equation with infinitely many solutions is still consistent!</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>If two linear systems have exactly the same solutions, they are called <term>equivalent</term>.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <activity xml:id="ex-1-1-forest-animals-3">
    <title>Yet another forest problem (U2-LO1, U2-LO2)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In another Wisconsin forest, there are deer and badgers. Together they have <m>18</m> heads and <m>56</m> legs. How many deer and badgers are in the forest? Is the resulting linear system consistent or inconsistent?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Let <m>x</m> be the number of deer, and <m>y</m> the number of badgers.</p>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Heads</title>
          <m>x + y = 18</m>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Legs</title>
          <m>4x + 4y = 56</m>
        </li>
      </dl>
      <p>From the second equation:</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>4(x + y) \amp = 56</mrow>
        <mrow>x + y \amp = 56/4 = 14</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        This system is inconsistent, since the first equation gives <m>x + y = 18</m> but the second gives <m>x + y = 14</m>. Try substituting the first equation into the second one.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  </introduction>

  <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-u2-geometric-examples">
    <title>Geometric examples in <m>\mathbb R^2</m> and <m>\mathbb R^3</m>: building intuition</title>

  <p>
    Recall that a line in the plane which passes through a point <m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m>, and has slope <m>m</m>, can be written in <em>point-slope form</em>, as a set of points <m>P(x,y)</m> which solve the equation
    <me>
      y - y_0 = m(x - x_0).
    </me>
  </p>

  <example>
    <title>The geometric point of view (U2-LO1, U2-LO2)</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the following three systems, inspired by our forest examples. Let's graph the lines defined by these equations and check how this reflects the number of solutions in each case.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          System 1
          <md>
            <mrow>x + y \amp = 18</mrow>
            <mrow>2x + 4y \amp = 56</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-u2-geometric-system-1">
          <caption>System 1: the two lines intersect at <m>(8,10)</m>.</caption>
          <image width="65%" xml:id="img-u2-geometric-system-1">
            <shortdescription>Two lines intersect at the unique solution (8,10).</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>The solid blue line is <m>x+y=18</m>, and the dashed orange line is <m>2x+4y=56</m>. On axes with equal scales, the lines cross at the marked point <m>(8,10)</m>, the system's only solution.</p>
            </description>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.24cm,y=0.24cm,>=Stealth,font=\small]
  \draw[step=5,gray!25,very thin] (0,0) grid (20,20);
  \draw[->,gray!70!black] (-1,0)--(21.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
  \draw[->,gray!70!black] (0,-1)--(0,21.5) node[left] {$y$};
  \foreach \tick in {5,10,15,20}{
    \draw (\tick,0.25)--(\tick,-0.25) node[below] {$\tick$};
    \draw (0.25,\tick)--(-0.25,\tick) node[left] {$\tick$};
  }
  \node[below left] at (0,0) {$0$};
  \begin{scope}
    \clip (0,0) rectangle (20,20);
    \draw[blue!75!black,line width=2.4pt] (-1,19)--(19,-1);
    \draw[orange!80!black,line width=1.3pt,dash pattern=on 5pt off 3pt] (-1,14.5)--(21,3.5);
  \end{scope}
  \fill[black] (8,10) circle (2pt);
  \node[above right,fill=white,inner sep=2pt] at (8,10) {$(8,10)$};
  \draw[blue!75!black,line width=2.4pt] (0,26)--(4,26);
  \node[right] at (5,26) {$x+y=18$};
  \draw[orange!80!black,line width=1.3pt,dash pattern=on 5pt off 3pt] (0,23.5)--(4,23.5);
  \node[right] at (5,23.5) {$2x+4y=56$};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          System 2
          <md>
            <mrow>x + y \amp = 18</mrow>
            <mrow>4x + 4y \amp = 56</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-u2-geometric-system-3">
          <caption>System 2: the two lines are parallel and distinct, so there is no solution.</caption>
          <image width="65%" xml:id="img-u2-geometric-system-3">
            <shortdescription>Two distinct parallel lines show that the system has no solution.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>The solid blue line <m>x+y=18</m> crosses the axes at <m>(18,0)</m> and <m>(0,18)</m>. The dashed orange line <m>4x+4y=56</m>, equivalently <m>x+y=14</m>, crosses them at <m>(14,0)</m> and <m>(0,14)</m>. Both lines have slope <m>-1</m>, so they never intersect.</p>
            </description>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.24cm,y=0.24cm,>=Stealth,font=\small]
  \draw[step=5,gray!25,very thin] (0,0) grid (20,20);
  \draw[->,gray!70!black] (-1,0)--(21.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
  \draw[->,gray!70!black] (0,-1)--(0,21.5) node[left] {$y$};
  \foreach \tick in {5,10,15,20}{
    \draw (\tick,0.25)--(\tick,-0.25) node[below] {$\tick$};
    \draw (0.25,\tick)--(-0.25,\tick) node[left] {$\tick$};
  }
  \node[below left] at (0,0) {$0$};
  \begin{scope}
    \clip (0,0) rectangle (20,20);
    \draw[blue!75!black,line width=2.4pt] (-1,19)--(19,-1);
    \draw[orange!80!black,line width=1.3pt,dash pattern=on 5pt off 3pt] (-1,15)--(15,-1);
  \end{scope}
  \draw[blue!75!black,line width=2.4pt] (0,26)--(4,26);
  \node[right] at (5,26) {$x+y=18$};
  \draw[orange!80!black,line width=1.3pt,dash pattern=on 5pt off 3pt] (0,23.5)--(4,23.5);
  \node[right] at (5,23.5) {$4x+4y=56$};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          System 3
          <md>
            <mrow>x + y \amp = 18</mrow>
            <mrow>4x + 4y \amp = 72</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-u2-geometric-system-2">
          <caption>System 3: the two lines coincide, giving infinitely many solutions.</caption>
          <image width="65%" xml:id="img-u2-geometric-system-2">
            <shortdescription>Two coincident lines show infinitely many solutions.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>The solid blue line <m>x+y=18</m> and the dashed orange line <m>4x+4y=72</m> lie exactly on top of one another. The orange dashes are drawn over the wider blue line so both remain visible. Every point on this common line is a solution.</p>
            </description>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.24cm,y=0.24cm,>=Stealth,font=\small]
  \draw[step=5,gray!25,very thin] (0,0) grid (20,20);
  \draw[->,gray!70!black] (-1,0)--(21.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
  \draw[->,gray!70!black] (0,-1)--(0,21.5) node[left] {$y$};
  \foreach \tick in {5,10,15,20}{
    \draw (\tick,0.25)--(\tick,-0.25) node[below] {$\tick$};
    \draw (0.25,\tick)--(-0.25,\tick) node[left] {$\tick$};
  }
  \node[below left] at (0,0) {$0$};
  \begin{scope}
    \clip (0,0) rectangle (20,20);
    \draw[blue!75!black,line width=2.4pt] (-1,19)--(19,-1);
    \draw[orange!80!black,line width=1.3pt,dash pattern=on 5pt off 3pt] (-1,19)--(19,-1);
  \end{scope}
  \draw[blue!75!black,line width=2.4pt] (0,26)--(4,26);
  \node[right] at (5,26) {$x+y=18$};
  \draw[orange!80!black,line width=1.3pt,dash pattern=on 5pt off 3pt] (0,23.5)--(4,23.5);
  \node[right] at (5,23.5) {$4x+4y=72$};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <conclusion>
      <p>
        System 1 has one intersection point: subtracting <m>x+y=18</m> from <m>x+2y=28</m> gives <m>y=10</m>, so <m>x=8</m>. System 2 gives <m>x+y=18</m> and <m>x+y=14</m>, so the two lines are parallel and the system has no solution. System 3 is the same line twice, since <m>4x+4y=72</m> is equivalent to <m>x+y=18</m>, so it has infinitely many solutions.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </example>

  <p>
    The same word <q>solution</q> also has a geometric meaning. A solution set can be a point, a line, a plane, empty, or higher-dimensional. In three dimensions, the solution set of a linear equation is a plane, provided at least one variable has a nonzero coefficient. To obtain an equation for such a plane, we take a different approach from the point-slope form used for lines in two dimensions. We use a normal vector, which points at right angles to the plane and thus gives a direction in which the plane <em>does not travel</em>.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-1-2-normal-vector-equation">
    <title>Normal vector equation for planes</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The plane passing through <m>P_0(x_0,y_0,z_0)</m> with <strong>normal vector</strong> <m>\mathbf{n}</m> is the set of all points <m>P(x,y,z)</m> such that <m>\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{x}_0</m> is orthogonal to <m>\mathbf{n}</m>. That is,
        <me>\mathbf{n} \cdot (\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{x}_0) = 0,</me>
        or in scalar form, if <m>\mathbf{n}=\begin{bmatrix}n_1\\n_2\\n_3\end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>\mathbf{x}_0=\begin{bmatrix}x_0\\y_0\\z_0\end{bmatrix}</m>,
        <me>n_1(x - x_0) + n_2(y - y_0) + n_3(z - z_0) = 0.</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        In general form, this equation is written as
        <me>ax+by+cz=d,</me>
        where <m>a=n_1</m>, <m>b=n_2</m>, <m>c=n_3</m>, and
        <me>d=n_1x_0+n_2y_0+n_3z_0.</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-1-2-normal-vector-toplane">
    <caption>A plane passing through a point <m>\mathbf{x}_0</m> with normal vector <m>\mathbf{n}</m>.</caption>
    <image width="60%" xml:id="img-1-2-normal-vector-to-plane">
      <shortdescription>
        A plane passing through a point with a given normal vector
      </shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          A pale blue parallelogram tilted upward to the right represents a plane, with a red point labeled <m>\mathbf{x}_0</m> lying on it. A thick arrow labeled <m>\mathbf{n}</m> starts at that point and points upward and to the left, perpendicular to the plane. A small right-angle marker near the base of the arrow indicates the perpendicular relationship.
        </p>
      </description>
      <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[
    rotate=20,
    >=Stealth,
    line cap=round,
    line join=round,
    plane/.style={draw=blue!65!black, fill=blue!12, thick},
    vector/.style={->, very thick},
    point/.style={circle, fill=red, inner sep=1.7pt}
]

\coordinate (P) at (0.35,0.05);

\draw[plane] (-2.0,-0.55) -- (1.8,-0.55) -- (2.65,0.65) -- (-1.15,0.65) -- cycle;
\node[blue!65!black] at (1.55,0.35) {plane};

\node[point, label={[yshift=4pt]below right:{$\mathbf{x}_0$}}] at (P) {};
\draw[vector] (P) -- (0.35,1.75) node[above] {$\mathbf{n}$};

\draw[thin] (0.55,0.05) -- (0.55,0.25) -- (0.35,0.25);

\end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <activity>
    <title>A plane from a point and a normal vector (U2-LO1)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the equation in general form of the plane passing through the point <m>P(1,0,3)</m>, normal to the vector <m>\mathbf{n}=\begin{bmatrix}2\\6\\4\end{bmatrix}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The equation describing the plane is
        <me>\mathbf{n}\cdot(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_0)=0,</me>
        which expands as
        <me>2(x - 1) + 6y + 4(z - 3) = 0</me>
        and simplifies to
        <me>2x + 6y + 4z = 14.</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <p>
    Shapes in three-dimensional space described as the zeros of a single equation are typically <em>two-dimensional</em>. Lines are described as points which satisfy <em>two</em> different equations.
    If a line in three-dimensional space is equal to the intersection of two planes passing through a point <m>\mathbf{x}_0</m> (see <xref ref="fig-1-2-intersection-of-two-planes"/>), with normal vectors <m>\mathbf{n}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{n}_2</m>, then the line is precisely the set of points <m>\mathbf{x}</m> which satisfy the two equations
    <me>
      \mathbf{n}_1 \cdot (\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{x}_0) = 0
    </me>
    and
    <me>
      \mathbf{n}_2 \cdot (\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{x}_0) = 0.
    </me>
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-1-2-intersection-of-two-planes">
    <caption>A line defined as the intersection of two planes, with normal vectors <m>\mathbf{n}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{n}_2</m>, passing through a common point <m>\mathbf{x}_0</m>.</caption>
    <image width="40%" xml:id="img-1-2-intersection-of-two-planes">
      <shortdescription>
        A line defined as the intersection of two planes passing through a common point.
      </shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          Two translucent parallelograms, one blue and one red, cross each other in space. Their overlap is a dark slanted line that passes through a red point labeled <m>\mathbf{x}_0</m>. Arrows labeled <m>\mathbf{n}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{n}_2</m> show normal directions for the two planes.
        </p>
      </description>
      <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[
    >=Stealth,
    line cap=round,
    line join=round,
    planeA/.style={draw=blue!65!black, fill=blue!18, thick},
    planeB/.style={draw=red!65!black, fill=red!15, thick},
    vector/.style={->, very thick},
    point/.style={circle, fill=red, inner sep=1.7pt}
]

\coordinate (L1) at (-1.55,-0.75);
\coordinate (L2) at (1.55,0.75);
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);

% Plane shapes.
\def\BluePlane{(-1.95,-0.05) -- (1.15,1.45) -- (1.95,0.05) -- (-1.15,-1.45) -- cycle}
\def\RedPlane{(-1.05,0.25) -- (2.05,1.75) -- (1.05,-0.25) -- (-2.05,-1.75) -- cycle}

% Above the line, blue should appear in front of red.
\begin{scope}
  \clip (L1) -- (L2) -- (4,3.2) -- (-4,3.2) -- cycle;
  \path[planeB] \RedPlane;
  \path[planeA] \BluePlane;
\end{scope}

% Below the line, keep the red plane in front.
\begin{scope}
  \clip (-4,-3.2) -- (4,-3.2) -- (L2) -- (L1) -- cycle;
  \path[planeA] \BluePlane;
  \path[planeB] \RedPlane;
\end{scope}

% Intersection line and labels.
\draw[very thick] (L1) -- (L2) node[above right] {$\ell$};
\node[point, label=above left:{$\mathbf{x}_0$}] at (P) {};

\draw[vector] (P) -- (-0.85,1.15) node[above] {$\mathbf{n}_1$};
\draw[vector] (P) -- (1.15,-0.9) node[below right] {$\mathbf{n}_2$};

\end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <definition xml:id="def-1-2-parametric-equation-for-line">
    <title>Parametric equation for a line</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The line through <m>P_0(x_0,y_0,z_0)</m> with <strong>direction vector</strong> <m>\mathbf{d}</m> is the set of points <m>P(x,y,z)</m> of the form
        <me>\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{x}_0+t\mathbf{d},</me>
        where <m>\mathbf{x}_0</m> is the vector corresponding to <m>P_0</m>. In coordinates, if <m>\mathbf{d}=\begin{bmatrix}d_1\\d_2\\d_3\end{bmatrix}</m>, this says
        <me>(x,y,z)=(x_0+td_1,y_0+td_2,z_0+td_3).</me>
        If <m>d_1,d_2,d_3</m> are all nonzero, we can also write the line <em>in symmetric form</em> as the set of points <m>P(x,y,z)</m> such that
        <me>
          \frac{x - x_0}{d_1} = \frac{y - y_0}{d_2} = \frac{z - z_0}{d_3}.
        </me>
        All three values in this equation are equal to the scalar <m>t</m> which occurs in the first equations.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-1-2-parametric-line">
    <caption>A line defined by a direction vector <m>\mathbf{d}</m> and a point <m>P_0</m>, with corresponding vector <m>\mathbf{x}_0</m>.</caption>
    <image width="100%" xml:id="img-1-2-parametric-line">
      <shortdescription>
        A line defined by a direction vector, a point, and its corresponding vector.
      </shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          A slanted black line passes through a red point labeled <m>P_0</m>. From that point, a red arrow labeled <m>\mathbf{d}</m> points along the line's direction. A nearby label <m>\mathbf{x}_0+t\mathbf{d}</m> marks the parametric form for points on the line.
        </p>
      </description>
      <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[
    >=Stealth,
    line cap=round,
    line join=round,
    point/.style={circle, fill=red, inner sep=1.7pt},
    direction/.style={->, very thick, red!70!black}
]

\coordinate (A) at (-2.3,-1.05);
\coordinate (B) at (2.3,1.05);
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);
\coordinate (D) at (1.25,0.57);

\draw[thick] (A) -- (B);
\node[below right] at (1.2,0.53) {$\mathbf{x}_0+t\mathbf{d}$};

\node[point, label=above left:{$P_0$}] at (P) {};
\draw[direction] (P) -- (D) node[midway, above] {$\mathbf{d}$};

\end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <activity>
    <title>A parametric line through two points (U2-LO1)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find a parametric equation for the line passing through the two points <m>(3,1,0)</m> and <m>(-3,1,4)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We may choose <m>\mathbf{d}</m> to be equal to
        <me>
          \begin{bmatrix}3\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}
          -
          \begin{bmatrix}-3\\1\\4\end{bmatrix}
          =
          \begin{bmatrix}6\\0\\-4\end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
        If we let <m>\mathbf{x}_0=\begin{bmatrix}3\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}</m>, then the parametric equation is
        <me>
          \mathbf{x}=
          \begin{bmatrix}3\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}
          +t\begin{bmatrix}6\\0\\-4\end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
        If we let <m>\mathbf{x}_0=\begin{bmatrix}-3\\1\\4\end{bmatrix}</m>, then we obtain a different parameterization, namely
        <me>
          \mathbf{x}=
          \begin{bmatrix}-3\\1\\4\end{bmatrix}
          +t\begin{bmatrix}6\\0\\-4\end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
        Both parameterizations define the same line. Because the second component of the direction vector is zero, a symmetric-equation form would need a separate caveat; the parametric form above is the clean description of the line.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <activity xml:id="act-u2-plane-perpendicular-line">
    <title>A plane perpendicular to a line (U2-LO1)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the scalar equation of the plane through <m>P=(4,2,1)</m> and perpendicular to the line
        <me>
          x=1+5t,\qquad y=-1+4t,\qquad z=2+3t.
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The direction vector of the line is
        <me>\mathbf{d}=\begin{bmatrix}5\\4\\3\end{bmatrix}.</me>
        Because the plane is perpendicular to the line, <m>\mathbf{d}</m> is a normal vector for the plane. Therefore
        <me>5(x-4)+4(y-2)+3(z-1)=0.</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <example xml:id="ex-u2-line-intersection-two-planes">
    <title>A line of intersection from two planes</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        We seek to find a parametric equation for the line of intersection of
        <me>
          x+y+z=1,\qquad -x+2y-3z=-1.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        First set <m>z=0</m>. Then
        <me>x+y=1,\qquad -x+2y=-1.</me>
        Adding the two equations eliminates <m>x</m> and gives <m>3y=1+(-1)=0</m>, so <m>y=0</m>. Substituting into the first equation gives <m>x=1-y=1</m>. Thus <m>P(1,0,0)</m> is on the line.
      </p>
      <p>
        Next set <m>z=1</m>. Then
        <me>x+y=0,\qquad -x+2y=2.</me>
        Adding the two equations eliminates <m>x</m> and gives <m>3y=0+2=2</m>, so <m>y=\frac23</m>. Substituting into the first equation gives <m>x=-y=-\frac23</m>. Thus <m>Q(-\frac23,\frac23,1)</m> is also on the line.
      </p>
      <p>
        Hence a direction vector is
        <me>
          \mathbf{v}=\overrightarrow{PQ}
          =\begin{bmatrix}-\frac23-1\\\frac23-0\\1-0\end{bmatrix}
          =\begin{bmatrix}-\frac53\\\frac23\\1\end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
        Thus one parametrization is
        <me>\mathbf{x}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\0\end{bmatrix}+t\begin{bmatrix}-\frac53\\\frac23\\1\end{bmatrix}.</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        This example shows how finding the intersection of planes leads naturally to solving systems of linear equations.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <warning xml:id="warn-u2-parallel-planes">
    <title>Parallel planes</title>
    <p>
      The method above assumes the two planes intersect in a line. If the normal vectors are parallel, then the planes are either identical or have no intersection.
    </p>
  </warning>

  </subsubsection>

  <conclusion>
  <p>
    In <xref ref="sec-u2-analyzing-linear-map"/>, the question <q>can this output be produced?</q> became a question about whether a system can be solved.
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="u2-act-revisit-target-output-equations">
    <title>Revisiting a requested output as equations (U2-LO1, U2-LO3)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Return to <xref ref="u2-act-preview-target-output"/>:
        <me>
          A=
          \begin{bmatrix}
          1\amp0\\
          0\amp1\\
          1\amp1
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
        Write <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m> for the two targets
        <me>
          \mathbf{b}_1=\begin{bmatrix}1\\2\\3\end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          \mathbf{b}_2=\begin{bmatrix}1\\2\\4\end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
        What equations do these two targets produce? Which target is reachable?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        For <m>\mathbf{b}_1</m>, the equations are
        <me>
          x_1=1,\qquad x_2=2,\qquad x_1+x_2=3.
        </me>
        These equations can all hold at the same time, so <m>\mathbf{b}_1</m> is reachable.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>\mathbf{b}_2</m>, the equations are
        <me>
          x_1=1,\qquad x_2=2,\qquad x_1+x_2=4.
        </me>
        The first two equations force <m>x_1+x_2=3</m>, not <m>4</m>. So <m>\mathbf{b}_2</m> is not reachable.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>
  </conclusion>

</subsection>
